---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import GeneticsSim from '../../simulators/genetics/GeneticsSim.astro';
import { geneticsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/genetics';

const toc = [
  { id: 'mendel', label: 'Mendel and his peas' },
  { id: 'words', label: 'Genes, alleles, genotypes and phenotypes' },
  { id: 'dominance', label: 'Dominant and recessive' },
  { id: 'punnett', label: 'Using a Punnett square' },
  { id: 'probability', label: 'Probability, not certainty' },
  { id: 'testcross', label: 'The test cross' },
  { id: 'dihybrid', label: 'Two traits at once' },
  { id: 'beyond', label: 'Beyond Mendel' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 12: Mendel’s Experiments and Heredity. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/12-introduction' },
  { text: 'Mendel, G. (1866). Versuche über Pflanzen-Hybriden [Experiments on plant hybridization]. Verhandlungen des naturforschenden Vereines in Brünn, 4, 3–47.' },
  { text: 'Griffiths, A. J. F., et al. (2020). Introduction to Genetic Analysis (12th ed.), Chapter 3: Single-Gene Inheritance. W. H. Freeman.' },
  { text: 'Miko, I. (2008). Gregor Mendel and the principles of inheritance. Nature Education, 1(1), 134.', url: 'https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/gregor-mendel-and-the-principles-of-inheritance-593/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="mendelian-genetics"
  description="Interactive Mendelian genetics simulator: choose parent genotypes for pea plants, build Punnett squares for monohybrid and dihybrid crosses, and breed offspring to compare predicted ratios with real chance."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={geneticsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Why do some traits skip a generation? In the 1860s a monk named Gregor Mendel answered this by breeding thousands of
      pea plants and counting the results. Choose the parents, predict their offspring with a Punnett square, then breed
      hundreds of plants to see how real chance compares with the prediction.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <GeneticsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>pea trait</strong> and the <strong>genotype of each parent</strong>, or press one of the classic crosses.</li>
      <li>Read the Punnett square: each parent’s possible gametes are along the edges, and each box is one equally likely offspring. <strong>Click</strong> a box or an allele to learn more.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Breed 1</strong>, <strong>Breed 10</strong> or <strong>Breed 100</strong>, or <strong>Start</strong> to breed continuously. Compare the filled bars (bred) with the outlines (predicted), and watch the graph.</li>
      <li>Switch to <strong>Two traits</strong> to cross seed shape and seed colour in a 4 × 4 Punnett square.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="mendel">Mendel and his peas</h2>
  <p>
    Gregor Mendel chose garden peas because they have clear either/or traits (purple or white flowers, round or wrinkled
    seeds), grow quickly and can be cross-pollinated by hand. He started with <strong>pure-breeding</strong> plants, crossed
    them, and counted the traits of the offspring over several generations. When he crossed purple-flowered and
    white-flowered plants, all the offspring were purple; but when those offspring were crossed together, white flowers
    reappeared in about one plant in four. He concluded that traits are passed on as separate “factors” that do not blend.
  </p>

  <h2 id="words">Genes, alleles, genotypes and phenotypes</h2>
  <p>
    Mendel’s factors are now called <strong>genes</strong>. A gene can come in different versions called
    <strong>alleles</strong>. Body cells carry two alleles of each gene, one from each parent. The pair of alleles is the
    <strong>genotype</strong>: <strong>homozygous</strong> if both are the same (PP or pp) and <strong>heterozygous</strong>
    if they differ (Pp). The characteristic you can observe, such as purple flowers, is the <strong>phenotype</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dominance">Dominant and recessive</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>dominant</strong> allele, written with a capital letter, shows its effect even when only one copy is present.
    A <strong>recessive</strong> allele, written in lower case, only shows when an individual has two copies. So PP and Pp
    plants both have purple flowers, and only pp plants are white. A heterozygous individual is a <strong>carrier</strong>:
    it hides the recessive allele but can pass it on, which is how traits seem to skip a generation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="punnett">Using a Punnett square</h2>
  <p>
    Each parent makes gametes carrying just one allele of each gene, and each allele has an equal chance of being passed
    on. This is Mendel’s <strong>law of segregation</strong>, explained by the separation of homologous chromosomes in
    meiosis. A <strong>Punnett square</strong> lists one parent’s possible gametes across the top and the other’s down the
    side; each box shows one possible offspring, and all boxes are equally likely. For Pp × Pp the boxes are PP, Pp, Pp and
    pp: a genotype ratio of 1 : 2 : 1 and a phenotype ratio of 3 purple : 1 white.
  </p>

  <h2 id="probability">Probability, not certainty</h2>
  <p>
    A Punnett square predicts <strong>probabilities</strong>, not exact outcomes. A ratio of 3 : 1 means each offspring has
    a 3 in 4 chance of being purple, not that every family of four will contain exactly one white plant. Each offspring is
    an independent event: two carrier parents who already have an affected child still have a 1 in 4 chance for the next
    one. With small numbers the results can differ a lot from the prediction; with large numbers they get closer, as the
    simulator’s graph shows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="testcross">The test cross</h2>
  <p>
    A purple plant could be PP or Pp. To find out, cross it with a homozygous recessive (pp) plant. If all offspring are
    purple, the plant is probably PP; if about half are white, it must be Pp.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dihybrid">Two traits at once</h2>
  <p>
    Mendel also followed two traits together, such as seed shape (round R, wrinkled r) and seed colour (yellow Y, green y).
    Crossing two RrYy plants gave about 9 round yellow : 3 round green : 3 wrinkled yellow : 1 wrinkled green. Each gene
    still gave 3 : 1, and the genes were inherited independently. This is the <strong>law of independent
    assortment</strong>, which holds for genes on different chromosomes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="beyond">Beyond Mendel</h2>
  <p>
    Many traits do not follow these simple patterns. In <strong>incomplete dominance</strong> heterozygotes are
    intermediate (red × white snapdragons give pink); in <strong>codominance</strong> both alleles show (the AB blood
    group). Genes close together on one chromosome are <strong>linked</strong>, and most human traits, such as height,
    depend on many genes and the environment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <p>
    Plant and animal breeders use these rules to produce crops and livestock with useful traits. Genetic counsellors use
    them to work out the chance of inherited conditions such as cystic fibrosis, which is recessive.
  </p>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Dominant means more common.”</strong> Dominance describes how alleles interact, not how frequent they are.</li>
    <li><strong>“A 3 : 1 ratio means exactly 3 out of every 4.”</strong> It is a probability for each offspring.</li>
    <li><strong>“Recessive alleles disappear.”</strong> They are hidden in carriers and can reappear in later generations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Genes come in alleles; the genotype is the pair of alleles, the phenotype is the trait shown.</li>
    <li>Dominant alleles show with one copy; recessive alleles need two.</li>
    <li>Punnett squares give the probability of each genotype and phenotype for every offspring.</li>
    <li>Large samples approach the predicted ratios; small samples vary by chance.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Model note: the simulator assumes complete dominance, equal chances for each allele and, in the dihybrid cross,
    genes on different chromosomes. Real inheritance can involve linkage, several genes and environmental effects. This is
    a learning tool, not a genetic risk calculator.
  </p>
</SimulatorLayout>
